Um, there are the cases new == old and new &gt; old. And all the more specific cases new == old + n. I haven’t seen the code so this is just speculation, but there are plenty of ways how an unexpected, &quot;can never happen&quot; comparison result causes immediate UB because there’s no execution path to handle it, causing garbage to be returned from a function (and if that garbage was supposed to be a pointer, well…) or even execution never hitting a  `ret` and just proceeding to execute whatever is next in memory.<p>Another super easy way to enter UB land by assuming an integer is nonnegative is array indexing.<p><pre><code>  int foo[5] = { … }
  foo[i % 5] = bar;
</code></pre>
Everything is fine as long as i isn’t negative. But if it is… (note that negative % positive == negative in C)
